ind that  voice  barack obama  thank you thank you  thank you all that's my best to say  thank you very much  thank you so much thank you  thank you  thank you thank you  that's i appreciate it thank you very  much  thank you so much  [Applause]  well  you know i know i made this speech about  there are no red states and blue states  but i suspect this state's kind of blue  just uh looking at that uh looking at  the crowd out here tonight  uh wha what a magnificent turnout i'm  i'm so  grateful to all of you for taking the  time out  of your busy schedules to to travel here  especially in new york traffic which i  just braved  um  you know i was mentioning that  when the book  that we're going to be discussing  tonight first came out which was in  1995-96  i had a much smaller  uh reception  but it  but this bookstore which had just opened  in fact was one of the stops that we  made and  it's wonderful to be back  uh close to 10 years later  and to see  how things have grown  so i i'm very grateful to to all of you  for being here  what i'm gonna do is i'm gonna just  spend a few minutes talking a little bit  about  uh  my perspective as it's expressed not  only in the book but also in the  campaign and and how those two things  connect and then we're gonna open it up  for some questions before we start to do  a book signing  um  you know i just had an appearance on on  charlie rose and you know he was asking  me how does  how does the book  connect with your politics  and it's very clear to me that there's a  direct line between the subject matter  that's contained in dreams from my  father and the type of politics that i  aspire to  because  essentially what this story is about  is  a boy  born to a father from kenya and a mother  from kansas  in hawaii  with an unusual name  who traveled to indonesia  came back  found himself in chicago  working in some of the lowest  income neighborhoods in the country  and then traveled back to africa  and somehow was able to w